<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oreste Baratieri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oreste Baratieri (1841–1901) was an Italian general who had fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s Thousand and became governor of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in the early 1890s. Commanding the Italian army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war">First Italo-Ethiopian War</a>, he was decisively defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> on 1 March 1896. The disaster ended his career, brought down the Crispi government and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oreste-baratieri.jpg" alt="Oreste Baratieri" width="227" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, but died more than 70 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oreste_Baratieri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Oreste Baratter on 13 November 1841 at Condino, in the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, and began as a volunteer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>'s Redshirts during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> of 1860 to 1861. After the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he joined the regular army and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-custoza-1866">Battle of Custoza</a> on 24 June 1866 and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mentana">Battle of Mentana</a> in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1874 he joined the Antinori geographical expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> for the Italian Geographical Society, and from 1876 to 1895 he sat for the Historical Left as deputy for Breno, in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>. As a colonel of the Bersaglieri he served in the colonial campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in 1887-88 and in 1890 and 1891. A general by 1891, he was made commander of Italian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the next year governor of Eritrea, and he defeated the Mahdists on the border, above all at the Battle of Kassala on 17 July 1894.</p><p>After the dispute with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a>, he reportedly promised crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to bring Menelik back in a cage, and in late 1895 he led 25,000 Italian troops and Eritrean Ascari into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. For nearly a year he avoided a decisive battle, until in February 1896 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a> ordered him to engage; it had already decided in secret to replace him with General Antonio Baldissera. At a conference at Sauria on 28 February he favoured a partial withdrawal, but his brigadiers and his orders pressed for an advance. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> his 9,894 men, outnumbered more than ten to one and advancing in four columns with poor maps, were separated and destroyed one after another.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> held him responsible for the defeats at Amba Alagi, Mekelle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> and accused him of abandoning his command in the retreat. Arrested on 21 March 1897 and tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>, he was acquitted but placed on the retired list. In his last years at Arco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> he wrote Memoirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in which he condemned European methods of subjugating Africa as inhumane and destructive. He died suddenly at Sterzing on 7 August 1901 and is buried at Arco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oreste Baratieri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Expansion soon collided with the independent Ethiopian Empire, and it ended in defeat at Adwa in 1896, where Emperor Menelik II routed the army of General <b>Oreste Baratieri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Condino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Sterzing, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.88,10.6&amp;year=1880" aria-label="Condino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Oreste Baratieri"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5627 6145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5627 6145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.88,10.6&amp;year=1880">What was here in 1880? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
